.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1252 PM PST Fri Nov 27 2026
(Sunday through next Thursday)

Rain returns to the forecast on Sunday as the upper ridge moves
east and an upstream low pressure moves into the PacNW. Warm
sector rain (more mellow) arrives NW to SE very early Sunday
across the region. Expect steadier rain to increase in coverage
and intensity late Sunday afternoon into early Sunday night. The
better dynamics associated with the cold front will lead to the
uptick in rain and possibility (15-20%) of thunderstorms. Greatest
threat for thunder remains over the North Bay and farther north.
Rainfall amounts have trended slightly upward: North Bay with 0.5"
to 1.1". The Santa Cruz Mountains and Santa Lucia Range will see
between 0.5" to 1.0" while the rest of the Bay Area and Central
Coast will see up to 0.25". The fropa will also cause an uptick
with winds - peak gusts between 25 to 35 mph. This will generally
be beneficial, but could cause travel impacts from wet roads.
Post frontal showers will linger Sunday night into Monday.

Still expecting a brief break before the next system arrives
Monday into Tuesday. As noted on the last discussion, system
number two continues to take a more southern track into SoCal.
We`ll be on the northern fringe, so we still have precip in the
forecast, but it is much less than previous forecast. In fact,
portions of the North Bay may only see a trace or nothing at all.
We did talk about concerns for the Central Coast, specifically
the burn areas, but even those trends are looking better. Less
impact potential. That being said, Tuesday is still a few days
away and the forecast can still change. Rain totals: North Bay
less than 0.05", the rest of the Bay Area will see 0.01- 0.1", and
the Central Coast will see 0.25-1.0". Wind threat may have
decreased as well for Tuesday and Wednesday, but still expecting
very windy conditions over the waters, immediate coastline, and
high terrain. Borderline for a Wind Advisory at this point.

Drier weather returns by Wednesday afternoon ridging building back
over the region.

Longer range forecast continues to show an active Pacific with
additional chances for precip.

