.BEACHES...
Issued at 1241 AM PST Fri Nov 27 2026

The combination of king tides, storm surge, and thermal expansion
from warm El Nino waters will bring coastal flooding along the
bayshore and portions of the Pacific Coast, despite lower
astronomical tides and limited wind- driven transport helping to
lessen the expected tidal levels from what we saw over the last
few days.

A Coastal Flood Advisory is in effect for bayshore locations
along the San Francisco, San Pablo, and southern Monterey Bays
through 5 PM Sunday. A Coastal Flood Advisory is in effect until
4 PM Saturday for the Pacific Coast. At the San Francisco tidal
gauge, high tide is expected to be 1.7 ft above normal (7.5 ft
MLLW) at 12:31 PM Friday, 1.6 ft above normal (7.4 ft MLLW) at
1:28 PM Saturday, and 0.9 ft above normal (6.8 ft MLLW) at 2:32 PM
Sunday. These predictions include up to 1.3 ft of surge that will
enhance the astronomical tide and flooding threat. High tide
varies up to 90 minutes earlier or later along the Pacific Coast
and through the San Francisco Bay, respectively.

A Beach Hazards Statement remains in effect through 6 AM Saturday due
to increased risk of sneaker waves, strong rip currents, and
breaking waves between 13 to 19 feet. These conditions will
coincide with King Tides and up to 1.5 ft of surge, bringing water
and waves farther up the beach than normal, restricting escape
from beaches and coastal areas. Large breaking waves can overpower
swimmers, pulling people into the sea from beaches, jetties, and
rocks resulting in significant physical injury and increasing the
risk of drowning. Inexperienced swimmers should remain out of the
water. Stay off of jetties, piers, and other waterside
infrastructure and never turn your back on the ocean!

Additional rounds of hazardous beach conditions are possible next
week as the storm door remains open.

